    <node id="&quot;1 \SIM N&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1 \sim n represents the continuous range of integers from 1 to n, which must be used exactly once to form a valid permutation in the problem."&lt;SEP&gt;"1 \sim n represents the range of integer values from 1 to n that must be used exactly once to form a valid permutation, defining the universe of possible elements for the problem."&lt;SEP&gt;"1 \sim n represents the universal set of integers from 1 to n, which must each appear exactly once in a valid permutation."&lt;SEP&gt;"1 \sim n represents the range of integers from 1 to n that must be used exactly once to form a valid permutation, defining the universe of valid values for the problem."&lt;SEP&gt;"1 \sim n represents the inclusive range of integer values (from 1 to n) that must appear exactly once in a valid permutation, forming the universal domain for element assignment."&lt;SEP&gt;"1 \sim n represents the inclusive numerical range of valid integers (from 1 to n) that must each appear exactly once in the permutation, forming the domain of possible values for the array elements."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0121bcb7315f1b6c1b1e1e1aa31da20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d945665931d7322be0eab1acd39ad26e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-545cb269f14609feb72aa54e1397a88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b66cd33bf18698f24c0d617867f8107&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d720ee12ae214beef8f6c0dc5728db8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff3b72123ab0d142f84a79cfdb9d4051</data>
    </node>
