    <edge source="&quot;STUDENT PROGRAM&quot;" target="&quot;INPUT_OUTPUT_AND_SEQUENTIAL_STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">208.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The student program implements sequential input reading and output of swap operations, directly applying the knowledge point of input/output and sequential structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"The student program reads input and outputs results, but uses exit(1) instead of structured output flow, violating proper input-output handling expected in competitive programming."&lt;SEP&gt;The Student Program, in relation to the INPUT_OUTPUT_AND_SEQUENTIAL_STRUCTURE knowledge point, consistently and correctly implements input/output handling and sequential execution principles using standard C++ functions such as std::cin and std::cout. It reads matrix data row-by-row and column-by-column in a structured, sequential manner, and outputs swap operations or other results in a clearly ordered, predictable flow that adheres to the expected interface of competitive programming problems. The program reliably manages multiple test cases, employs loop structures to process input sequences, and ensures that all output is generated in strict sequence following input consumption. Multiple evaluations, including a Chinese-language assessment confirming that “the student program correctly implements the input/output flow, sequentially reading n and m and outputting results,” validate that the program’s I/O mechanism and control flow are properly structured and functionally sound.

While several descriptions note that the program produces invalid or incorrect output—due to flawed logic in matrix processing, determinant calculation, or swap operation sequencing—these errors are consistently attributed to deficiencies in the underlying algorithmic or computational logic, not to failures in input/output handling or sequential execution structure. The program’s adherence to the INPUT_OUTPUT_AND_SEQUENTIAL_STRUCTURE is therefore not compromised by these computational flaws; rather, they exist independently at the algorithmic layer. In summary, the Student Program fully satisfies the requirements of the INPUT_OUTPUT_AND_SEQUENTIAL_STRUCTURE knowledge point by demonstrating correct, reliable, and well-structured sequential processing of input and output, even though its overall functionality may be impaired by non-I/O-related algorithmic errors.</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-cf3655e2f9ff0ad97a40cbb5e827990f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd98342443974d00046bbc62a7ae1f50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5c14791229c01755e1e377ce7b97c74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a980f956dbb2d415bf61fa89ad97a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba8082d2aafc7f8fc80c51b5821c2ae7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b80c66cbff74b6030244a0523bb9e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ad12dbd91d84d77d978a49fdce06b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d6ae6c968489276c8bb614966bd0f46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a78168579a4a01a20c4871cdaa1bbe59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bfafcf2098d0e8657e26e5ca39cfbc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf205584a12138b86107f950268b071f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f7b94d5736abea4b1f3639f0bda773f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4bd4c7c33601a905c773b5092cf3a9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4524936a6b8c2ae52a229c861568c00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167bf23db68d82578bef6b617284955f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f97b7273e9cd49bbbd3b2dcef862908b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49adf7c339e42e84130e12d95429c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e4b814718769c7c5ff34fd8d9992e16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7505a8a58bc2ae0a0f49a4f4a99ca45c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7559a33bedc5f901d766c18542da6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f273c58705b21c2ef1fc6c43ff66b9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246822371b4a88626131f0e16a7011d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acfb1c8a2a7c5ab2297cc1bce76d8554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60231925bd696a34976f33351b0f3da8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cd35d2da3ec05d7b2e867504beda050&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af63a0c7cd3b8a431db6d128e0ad90a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59b725cdca1ae7922077ce5da4c129a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3885e6ab1ee62defc95a4d0557b1cf8d</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;STUDENT PROGRAM&quot;" target="&quot;CONTROL_STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">116.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Student Program 使用 exit(1) 异常终止程序，而非正常输出 'NO'，违反控制结构规范，属于 Bad: [错误使用程序终止]。"&lt;SEP&gt;"The Student Program relies on nested loops and conditional updates (cnti, cntj) to control movement, but contains logical errors in these control structures that break the intended algorithm."&lt;SEP&gt;"The program's flawed logic in loop control and variable updates (cnti, cntj) reveals a misunderstanding of control structures."&lt;SEP&gt;The Student Program relies on control structures—including nested loops, while-loops, for-loops with step increments, and if-else conditionals—to manage program flow, handle multiple test cases, adjust the value of variable e based on mathematical constraints (particularly x and x % 3 or x % -3), track coordinates, and perform operations such as swaps and extremal value updates. While the structural implementation of these control mechanisms (e.g., loop syntax, use of std::max/min, and loop nesting) is often correctly applied, the conditional logic within them is fundamentally flawed. Specifically, the student’s if-else logic for determining e based on x’s value and its modulo with 3 (or -3) contains critical errors in edge case handling, such as negative x values, non-zero remainders, and integer division behavior. These flaws lead to incorrect determinant values across a range of inputs, revealing a poor mastery of control flow in the context of mathematical constraints. The program’s attempts to use control structures to ensure the determinant equals x are undermined by incorrect condition formulation, indicating a misunderstanding of how control structures must be precisely aligned with arithmetic properties to guarantee correctness. Although the program demonstrates attempted application of key programming constructs, the persistent logical errors in condition evaluation prevent reliable or accurate output, highlighting a gap in the student’s ability to translate mathematical requirements into robust, error-free control flow.&lt;SEP&gt;"学生程序使用 if、for、return void() 等控制结构处理边界条件和逻辑分支，体现了对控制结构的基本掌握。"&lt;SEP&gt;"The student's use of exit(1) for m==1 breaks the program flow and ignores the requirement to output 'NO' for invalid cases, showing poor control structure design."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-3885e6ab1ee62defc95a4d0557b1cf8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304a22c33987a0af2f7be4a2bf524b1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a980f956dbb2d415bf61fa89ad97a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b80c66cbff74b6030244a0523bb9e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5c14791229c01755e1e377ce7b97c74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f7b94d5736abea4b1f3639f0bda773f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4bd4c7c33601a905c773b5092cf3a9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a78168579a4a01a20c4871cdaa1bbe59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba8082d2aafc7f8fc80c51b5821c2ae7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60231925bd696a34976f33351b0f3da8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cd35d2da3ec05d7b2e867504beda050&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af63a0c7cd3b8a431db6d128e0ad90a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167bf23db68d82578bef6b617284955f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d59b725cdca1ae7922077ce5da4c129a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d49adf7c339e42e84130e12d95429c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f97b7273e9cd49bbbd3b2dcef862908b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ad12dbd91d84d77d978a49fdce06b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d6ae6c968489276c8bb614966bd0f46</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;STUDENT PROGRAM&quot;" target="&quot;SIMULATION&quot;">
      <data key="d3">195.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The "STUDENT PROGRAM" exhibits a pervasive and systemic cognitive bias toward "SIMULATION"—a flawed computational thinking pattern in which the student consistently defaults to mechanical, step-by-step modeling as a universal problem-solving strategy, even in contexts where such an approach is mathematically invalid, computationally infeasible, or unnecessarily inefficient. This bias is not an isolated technique but a deeply ingrained behavioral tendency that undermines algorithmic correctness, mathematical rigor, and computational efficiency across diverse tasks.

In the context of string construction, the "STUDENT PROGRAM" simulates the process by逐字符填充数组 (filling an array character by character) to approximate a defined notion of "beauty = m." However, the algorithm fails to correctly simulate this condition, particularly when n - m &gt; 26 or m == 1, indicating a fundamental misunderstanding of the problem’s constraints and the combinatorial limits of character allocation. This same simulation pattern is applied indiscriminately to other domains, revealing a consistent pattern of misapplication.

In a linear algebra task, the student attempted to compute a target determinant value for a 3x3 matrix by fixing six elements and dynamically calculating a seventh element, denoted as 'e,' to satisfy a constraint. However, the arithmetic logic used to derive 'e' was algebraically incorrect, failing to properly implement the cofactor expansion of the determinant formula, thereby rendering the simulation incapable of guaranteeing the determinant would equal the target value 'x' for all valid integer inputs. This exposes a critical gap in understanding the interdependent relationships among matrix entries and the mathematical foundations of determinants.

In a second instance, the student implemented a simulation of adjacent swaps to reposition '1's within a 2D grid, aiming to concentrate them in the top-left quadrant. While the conceptual intent to model local transformations was appropriate, the implementation was deeply flawed: it employed a naive brute-force approach with incorrect sequencing, inadequate boundary handling, and insufficient tracking of valid move sequences. The method lacked heuristic guidance, state-space optimization, or any principled use of constraint satisfaction or pathfinding algorithms—despite the problem being well-suited for more efficient algorithmic solutions such as greedy placement or dynamic programming.

In a third failure, the student enumerated all possible subarrays to solve a problem requiring aggregate state tracking, such as computing maxima or minima. Though superficially aligned with simulation, this approach was computationally intractable, exhibiting exponential time complexity, and suffered from poor</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-304a22c33987a0af2f7be4a2bf524b1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf3655e2f9ff0ad97a40cbb5e827990f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd98342443974d00046bbc62a7ae1f50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5c14791229c01755e1e377ce7b97c74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a980f956dbb2d415bf61fa89ad97a2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba8082d2aafc7f8fc80c51b5821c2ae7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a78168579a4a01a20c4871cdaa1bbe59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d6ae6c968489276c8bb614966bd0f46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ad12dbd91d84d77d978a49fdce06b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3885e6ab1ee62defc95a4d0557b1cf8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bfafcf2098d0e8657e26e5ca39cfbc6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf205584a12138b86107f950268b071f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f7b94d5736abea4b1f3639f0bda773f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4524936a6b8c2ae52a229c861568c00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-167bf23db68d82578bef6b617284955f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f97b7273e9cd49bbbd3b2dcef862908b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e4b814718769c7c5ff34fd8d9992e16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7505a8a58bc2ae0a0f49a4f4a99ca45c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f7559a33bedc5f901d766c18542da6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f273c58705b21c2ef1fc6c43ff66b9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acfb1c8a2a7c5ab2297cc1bce76d8554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60231925bd696a34976f33351b0f3da8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af63a0c7cd3b8a431db6d128e0ad90a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4bd4c7c33601a905c773b5092cf3a9a</data>
